# Cal Coherence — Edge Function secrets, LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the same directory and fill in the real value:
#
#     cp supabase/functions/.env.example supabase/functions/.env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. This `.env.example` is committed and must never contain a
# real key.
#
# ── Why the key lives here and not in the app ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Anything shipped in an iOS binary is readable. An `.ipa` is a zip; strings are
# recoverable in minutes, and there is no obfuscation that changes that. A key
# extracted from the app is a key someone else can spend, on our account, with no
# rate limit and no attribution. So the app never holds one: it calls our own Edge
# Function, and the function holds the key (ARCHITECTURE.md §8.1, §10).
#
# That is also why `CoachClient`'s doc comment says every call behind it goes to
# our function rather than to a provider directly.
#
# ── Local vs hosted ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# LOCAL (what works today):
#     supabase functions serve --env-file supabase/functions/.env
#
# HOSTED (once a Supabase project exists and `supabase link` has been run):
#     supabase secrets set OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# or  supabase secrets set --env-file supabase/functions/.env
#
# `supabase secrets set` writes to the *linked* project, so it needs a real
# project first. Until then this file is the only place the key goes.

OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-replace-me

# The model, kept in configuration rather than hardcoded so it can be changed
# without a deploy. Decided: gpt-5.6-luna.
CAL_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna

# ── Retrieval (M1) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OPTIONAL. With CHROMA_URL unset, the function skips retrieval entirely and
# behaves exactly as it did before — so a fresh checkout works with no vector
# database and no seeding step.
#
# The corpus is Dr. Mia's practices, the ten coherence questions, and the 231
# campus places. It holds NO personal data: it is identical for every student,
# which is why it can be a shared collection with no per-user isolation. Personal
# memory is a separate collection with different rules and is blocked on real
# authentication — a metadata filter is not access control when the client picks
# the filter value.
#
# Local:
#     docker run -d --name cal-chroma -p 8000:8000 chromadb/chroma:latest
#     ./tools/build-content-corpus.py     # free, offline, writes content/corpus.jsonl
#     ./tools/embed-corpus.py --dry-run   # what it would cost
#     ./tools/embed-corpus.py             # embed + upsert
#     ./tools/eval-retrieval.py           # prove it returns the right chunks
#
# Then serve the function with CHROMA_URL set. Note that `supabase functions
# serve` runs the function inside Docker, so localhost is not the host: use
# http://host.docker.internal:8000.
#
# CHROMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8000
# CHROMA_TOKEN=            # hosted Chroma only; local needs none
# CAL_COLLECTION=cal_content
# CAL_EMBED_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
